                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            – and operations on them, but also formalizes the idea that each such term is of some definite
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : \mathbb{N}
            is different from the type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
            between natural numbers.
          %p
            Explicitly, type theory is a formal language, essentially a set of rules for rewriting certain strings of symbols, that describes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/introduction+rule"} introduction
            of types and their terms, and computations with these, in a sensible way.
          %p What may seem like a triviality on first sight turns out to have deep implications:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %strong> foundations of mathematics
                \. On the one hand,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
                itself is subsumed in the plain idea of operations on terms of types, by observing that any type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                may be thought of as the type of terms satisfying some
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
                = succeed "-paradigm" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} propositions are types
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
                of the proposition is nothing but a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
                of the corresponding type. This identification leads to a very fruitful identification of operations on types with logical operations.
            %li
              %p
                %strong> programming language
                \. Since such a proof is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
                %em program
                whose output is a certain type. Under this
                = succeed "-paradigm," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proofs+as+programs"} proofs as programs
                = succeed "." do
                  %em programming language
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coq"} Coq
                and
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Agda"} Agda
                are concrete machine implementations of such a language. They are used both in computer science, where the typing provides
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/certified+programming"} certified programming
            %li
              %p
                %strong> calculus for category theory
                \. On the other hand, if one regards, as is natural, any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t : X
                to exist in a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                of other terms
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi Γ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : \Gamma
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
                is naturally identified with a “map”
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo :
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t : \Gamma \to X
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
                of type theory are identified, respectively, with the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} formal language for speaking about categories
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} Elementary Theory of the Category of Sets
          %p
            These aspects naturally harmonize, involving, reflecting on, and subsuming
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of categories and
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            in categories.
          %p
            Moreover, when following the idea of type theory through seriously, it turns out to go well beyond these topics even: If all logical statements are to be witnessed by terms of the type that corresponds to the given proposition, then this should notably be true for what is maybe the most basic logical notion, that of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y : X
            of a type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            the existence of an
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_X(x,y)
            which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} represents
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equal
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : Id_X(x,y)
            is a proof of this fact. But this idea necessarily iterates, with the equality of two such proofs in turn being witnessed by a term of a second order identity type, and so on. Reflecting on this shows that the type-theoretic notion of equality resulting this way is not the traditional one, but is the notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} equivalence in an (∞,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            properly implemented is thus called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
          %p
            Notice that this is obtained not by adding something to the basic idea of type theory, but by removing something, namely the ad-hoc assumption of definite
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
          %p
            Similarly, while plain vanilla type theory formalizes
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+mathematics"} intuitionistic mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/law+of+excluded+middle"} law of excluded middle
            to make type theory also describe
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
          %h2#CategoricalSemantics As a formal language for category theory
          %p
            One way to look at type theory, from the point of view of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theorist
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
                for describing the
                %span.newWikiWord
                  construction
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/type+constructor"} ?
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                in a category
            %a{:href => "#Taylor"} Taylor
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "http://www.paultaylor.eu/ASD/foufct/cattype.html"} section 2
          %p
            This interpretation can be called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            between type theories and categories
          %p
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+contexts"} category of contexts
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Con
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi Lan
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi TypeTheories
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mi Con
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mi Lan
                    %mi Categories
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \:  (Con \dashv Lan) :
                    TypeTheories
                    \\stackrel{\overset{Lan}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{Con}{\to}}
                    Categories
          %p
            This is discussed in detail at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
          %p
            There are many different versions of this adjunction, depending on what kind of type theory we consider (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Con
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con
                assigns to a type theory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+contexts"} category of contexts
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                (a review is for instance in
                %a{:href => "#Taylor"} Taylor
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a{:href => "www.paultaylor.eu/ASD/foufct/cattype.html"} section 2.8
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Lan
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lan
                assigns to a category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                a canonically defined type theory, called its
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
                (a review is for instance in
                %a{:href => "#Taylor99"} Taylor 99
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.cs.man.ac.uk/~pt/Practical_Foundations/html/s76.html"} section 7.6
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is equivalently a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Con
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)\to C
            or a morphism of type theories
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi Lan
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to Lan(C)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            has a tautological model in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            there is a tautological model of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Lan
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lan(C)
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Lan
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lan(C)
            and how it is interpreted in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            via this tautological model. We will do this in this section somewhat informally; in the next section we give a more formal definition of type theories.
          %p A couple of side notes for experts:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                1-categorical semantics is only relevant to
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} extensional
                type theory; see the section on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional type theory
                vs.
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} extensional type theory
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#ExtensionalIntensional"} below
            %li
              %p
                The description given below is a somewhat simplified one, in that we identify objects of the category with single types, rather than contexts. This is strictly speaking only okay in type theories with a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
                operation, which allows us to reinterpret every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                as a single type. An alternative approach which avoids this problem is to use
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+multicategories"} cartesian multicategories
          %h3#TheInternalLanguageOfACategory The internal language of a category
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %strong internal language
            as a type theory (see e.g. in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Taylor"} Taylor
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.paultaylor.eu/ASD/foufct/cattype.html"} section 2.8
          %p
            There is a whole page on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntactic
            constructs corresponding to
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"}> type
              s
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            denote
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            to indicate
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain"} domain
          %h4#types_terms_and_variables Types, terms, and variables
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain"} domain
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain"} codomain
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is in symbols
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi f
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \stackrel{f}{\to} A\,.
            %li
              %p
                In the internal language of the category the same is a
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)
                of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                is a
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+variable"} free variable
                of
                %em type
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi B
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\colon B \vdash f(x)\colon A\,.
          %p
            We may think of the
            %em free variables
            here as being placeholders for all the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+element"} generalized element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi x
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \stackrel{x}{\to} B
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\colon B \vdash f(x)\colon A
            indicates that with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \stackrel{f}{\to} A
            given we may send
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi x
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \stackrel{x}{\to} B
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi x
                    %mi B
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (U \stackrel{x}{\to} B \stackrel{f}{\to}A ) = (U \stackrel{f(x)}{\to} A)
          %p
            So the notation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\colon B \vdash f(x)\colon A
            is a direct reflection of the description of the morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Func
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \hookrightarrow Func(C^{op}, Set)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          %h4#evaluation Evaluation
          %p
            Generally,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            of morphisms in the category
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %mi B
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi g
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi f
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C \stackrel{f}{\to} B \stackrel{g}{\to} A
                  \\;\;
                  \=
                  \\;\; C \stackrel{g \circ f }{\to} A
          %p
            corresponds to
            %em substitution
            in type theory of a term for a free variable: the morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            are interpreted as terms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(y)
            of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            respectively, where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            are variables of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            respectively. The composite morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\circ f
            is the term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(f(x))
            of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is again a variable of type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p In symbols this is written as:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi C
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi B
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi C
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac { x\colon C \vdash f(x)\colon B \qquad y\colon B \vdash g(y)\colon A }
                  { x\colon C \vdash g(f(x))\colon A }
          %p
            Here the horizontal bar indicates that we have written down a
            = succeed "," do
              %em rule
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgement"} judgement
            on the bottom is valid whenever the judgements on the top are valid.
          %p
            What is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphism"} identity morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mi A
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \stackrel{f = Id_A}{\to} A
            in category theory is a term representing the function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) = x
            in type theory, namely the variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            itself regarded as a term:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is a term of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is a variable of type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p In symbols:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac {}{ x\colon A \vdash x\colon A }
          %h4#TypeConstructors Type constructors
          %p
            What sorts of additional syntactical constructions you allow on types and terms corresponds to the structure of the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            in which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            is intended to occur.
          %p
            For example, if our semantic categories have binary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            %em type constructor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ×
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times
            allowing us to build a new
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+type"} product type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B
            from two given types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            It will also have
            %em term constructors
            allowing us to build, for example, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle a,b\rangle
            of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B
            from any given terms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            of type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(z)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2(z)
            from any term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            of type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1\langle a,b\rangle = a
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2 \langle a,b\rangle = b
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \pi_1(z),\pi_2(z)\rangle = z
          %p
            Note the great advantage of the type-theoretic formalism: the notation (and thought process) can be very set-theoretic, but because the terms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            can denote morphisms with arbitrary domain (i.e. generalized elements), this really describes the full universal property of a categorical cartesian product.
          %h4#dependent_types Dependent types
          %p
            An important extension of type theory involves
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"}> dependent type
              s
            \: types which are a “function” of the
            %em elements
            of some other type. For instance, the type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(m)
            of “days of the month” is a function of the element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            of the type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            of months, since different months have different allowable collections of days.
          %p
            In category theory language a
            %em
              type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(x)
              dependent on
            an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is (again) a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mi p
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  C
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow\mathrlap{p}
                  \\\
                  A
                  }
          %p
            thought of as an
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"} bundle
            of objects. In type theory language this is written
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x : A \vdash C(x) : Type
          %p
            and read “
            %em>
              for each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              of type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              there is a type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(x)
            ” or “
            %em>
              for each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              of type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              there is a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(x)
              of type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type
            \.”
          %p
            Here the variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is again a placeholder for
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+element"} generalized element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash
            denotes not postcomposition with a morphism, but
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            along a morphism: for every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+element"} generalized element
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi x
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \stackrel{x}{\to} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mo *
                  %mi C
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^* C := C(x)
            in
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mi p
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi U
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi x
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  C(x) &amp;\to&amp; C
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow\mathrlap{p}
                  \\\
                  U &amp;\stackrel{x}{\to}&amp; A
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            This
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(x)
            is the type (relative to the domain of definition
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            at the parameter value
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            (which also has domain of definition
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          %p
            If we have a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h : A\to D
            regarded as a term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A \vdash h(a):D
            (rather than as a generalized element of
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            functor on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/overcategories"} overcategories
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi h
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  h^* : \mathcal{C}/D \to \mathcal{C}/A
                  \\,.
          %p
            represents the “reindexing” or “substitution” operation: a dependent type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y:D\vdash C(y):Type
            gives rise to a dependent type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A \vdash C(h(x)):Type
          %p
            Now the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            of this pullback functor always exists, and is given by postcomposition with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : C \to A
            (representing a dependent type
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A \vdash C(x):Type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi h
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C  \stackrel{h(p)}{\to} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = *
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with all its fibers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(x)
            and regards it as an independent type, i.e. an object of the category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
            and written
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\sum_{x : A} C(x)
          %p
            This is another
            %em type constructor
            that constructs the new type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{x : A} C(x)
            from the dependent type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A\vdash C(x)
          %p
            Now by the universal property of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  z \;:\; \sum_{x : A} C(x)
          %p of this sum type, i.e. a morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  z : U \to C
          %p
            determines a morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \coloneqq p(z)\colon U\to A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mo *
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^*C
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y:C(x)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi U
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi z
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi Id
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mi p
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi U
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mo :
                              %mo =
                              %mi p
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi z
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  U &amp;\stackrel{z}{\to}&amp; C
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{Id}} &amp;&amp; \downarrow\mathrlap{p}
                  \\\
                  U &amp;\stackrel{x := p(z)}{\to}&amp; A
                  }
          %p
            Thus, we can think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = \sum_{x : A } C(x)
            as the the
            %em type of pairs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,y)
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : A
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y : C(x)
          %p
            Similarly, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            functor is, if it exists, the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
            operation , which sends the dependent type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : C \to A
            to the type
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Pi_{x:A} C(x)
          %p
            regarded as the
            %em type of functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            such that for any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) : C(x)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %h3#logic_versus_type_theory_in_categorical_semantics Logic versus type theory in categorical semantics
          %p
            How does type theory relate to logic? Well,
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+logic"} propositional logic
            is just the type theory whose semantic categories are
            = succeed "." do
              %em posets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Q
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P,Q,\dots
            are usually called
            = succeed "," do
              %em propositions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P\le Q
            (or, in more logical language, “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            implies
            = succeed "\u201D)." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            and called “and,” the type constructor for binary coproducts is usually written
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∨
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
            and called “or,” and so on. The term constructors are generally called
            = succeed "," do
              %em inference rules
          %p
            Now, it turns out that there are (at least) two ways to reconcile propositional logic (the type theory of posets) with type theory of more general categories, producing
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} predicate logic
          %h4#logic_over_type_theory Logic over type theory
          %p
            In the first approach, which can be described as
            %em typed predicate logic
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %em logic over type theory
          %p
            In this case, the syntax has collections of types and terms, together with constructors, and also rules for forming propositions out of types and terms, and inference rules for forming implications between propositions. The types and terms form the underlying type theory of the logic, and the propositions ‘depend’ on these. For instance, given two terms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y
            of type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x=y
            which asserts that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            are equal. Other important “proposition constructors” are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantifiers"} quantifiers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∃
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists x
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is a variable associated to a type (not a proposition). This can concisely be formalized as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+type+system"} pure type system
            with one sort for types and another sort for propositions, such that propositions are allowed to depend on types, but not conversely.
          %p
            The natural home for the semantics of typed predicate logic turns out to be an
            = succeed ":" do
              %em indexed poset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            together with a functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Pos
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P:C^{op}\to Pos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            of propositions that is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} fibred over
            the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            of terms, and whose fibers are posets. (Thus, an alternative way of thinking of propositional logic is as the ‘logic’ of a poset fibred over the trivial one-object category, which corresponds to the fact that the propositions do not contain or depend on typed terms.) The ordinary type theory happens in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            as described above, and a proposition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            with a free variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is interpreted by an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\phi]
            of the poset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(A)
            (the fiber over
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Pos
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P:Set^{op}\to Pos
            sending each set to the poset of its
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\phi]
            as “the set of all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in A
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x)
            is true.” Another way of describing this setup is as the
            %em subobject fibration
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi cod
                    %mo :
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cod:Sub(C)\to C
          %p
            Just as the allowed constructions on types are reflected in the structure of the semantic category, the allowed constructions on propositions here are reflected in the structure of the semantic posets
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(A)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            of propositions, then each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(A)
            must be a meet-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semilattice"}> semilattice
            \. The action of the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            on morphisms, usually written
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f^*:P(Y)\to
                    P(X)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            for the variable of a proposition, multiple variables and substitutions being interpreted by means of finite products, as in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi π
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi^*:P(X)\to
                  P(X\times Y)
            interprets the adding of an unused variable to a context. The left and right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoints
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi f
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f \dashv f^* \dashv \forall_f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_\pi
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_\pi
            interpret the traditional existential and universal quantifiers.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of various sorts of categories are most naturally regarded as the typed predicate logic associated to the “poset of subobjects” functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Pos
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub:C^{op}\to Pos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            induce the required semantic structure on both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sub
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(X)
            is a meet-semilattice and the adjoints
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f
            exist, while if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+category"} Heyting category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sub
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(X)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
            and both adjoints
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_f
            exist. See also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tripos"} tripos
          %h4#propositions_as_types Propositions as types
          %p
            The second approach to reconciling type theory with logic is to blur the distinction between types and propositions; this is called the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"}> propositions as types
            ” paradigm. Instead of requiring that a proposition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            be interpreted as merely true or false (that is, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+value"} truth value
            or equivalently a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingleton"} subsingleton
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\phi]
            is the set of
            = succeed "," do
              %em proofs
            = succeed "," do
              %em reasons
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is true; it is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
            iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is true, but a true statement may have many distinct proofs (although, for technical reasons, this is not the case in naive categorical models of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi ψ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\phi\Rightarrow
                    \\psi
            %em type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \to \psi
            of all proofs that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            implies
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            actually does imply
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall
            become identified with the dependent type constructors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
          %p
            In this case, the semantics involved is the more general
            %em codomain fibration
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:C^\to\to C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+categories"} slice categories
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C/A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pos
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pos(C/A)
            of these slice categories (in which all parallel morphisms are identified). Note also that the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pos
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pos(C/A)
            is equivalent to the poset of subobjects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+exact+completion"} free exact completion
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %h3#syntactic_categories_and_free_models Syntactic categories and free models
          %p
            As mentioned above, there are two equivalent ways to describe formally the semantics of a given type theory (possibly with logic) in a category. There is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            (which is at least sometimes an
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi type
                  %mi theories
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %munderover
                    %mo ⇄
                    %mi Lan
                    %mi Con
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi categories
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} type theories \quad \underoverset{Lan}{Con}{\rightleftarrows} \quad categories
          %p in which
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the right adjoint
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Lan
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lan
                (sometimes called “semantics”) assigns to a category its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal type theory
                whose types and terms (and propositions, if present) are the objects and morphisms (and subobjects) of the category, while
            %li
              %p
                the left adjoint
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Con
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con
                (sometimes called “syntax”) builds the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} syntactic category
                of a type theory, whose objects, morphisms, and subobjects are the types (or contexts), terms, and propositions of the type theory.
          %p
            Thus, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a type theory and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            a category with corresponding structure, it is equivalent to give a structure-preserving functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Con
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con(T) \to C
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi Lan
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\to Lan(C)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Con
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Con
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Lan
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lan
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
          %p
            By the way, it should be noted that there are various technical difficulties in making this precise. For instance, categories of any sort form a 2-category (or something more, if they are higher categories themselves), so we have to either make type theories into a 2-category as well, or consider strict categorical structures that form a 1-category. Also, there is a bit of a mismatch in that
            %em substitution
            in type theory is usually “implicit,” which implies that it is strictly associative, but the corresponding categorical operation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            is not generally strictly associative. For this reason, various people have defined technical intermediaries between type theories and categories, which mostly boil down to a category equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+fibration"} split fibration
            replacing its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain+fibration"} codomain fibration
            = succeed "," do
              %em comprehension category
            = succeed "," do
              %em category with attributes
            = succeed ";" do
              %em contextual category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+model+of+dependent+types"} categorical model of dependent types
          %h2#Syntax Syntax of type theory
          %p It’s hard to give a universal definition of “a type theory,” but the following very general setup covers most cases.
          %p
            Generally, a type theory is formulated by the rules called
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %strong>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+formation"} type formation
                  rules
                , which say on which basis a new
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
                can be introduced
            %li
              %p
                %strong>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+introduction"} term introduction
                  rules
                , which say how that new type can be inhabited by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            %li
              %p
                %strong>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+elimination"} term elimination
                  rules
                , which say how from a term of the new type one gets terms of other types
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation+rules"} computation rules
                which constrain the result of combining term introduction with term elimination.
          %p
            Note that in general, the following definitions are mutually
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursion"} recursive
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong typing declaration
                is something of the form
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t:A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
                is a
                %strong term
                (of type
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is the
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong type
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Type
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                is a list of typing declarations, in which each term is a fresh variable (i.e. one not occurring to the left of its typing declaration). If the list of types is not fixed, then one requires that each type occurring in a context be well-formed relative to the sub-context appearing to its left. In other words, for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma, x:A
                to be a valid context, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
                (see below)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash A:Type
                must be derivable.
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
                or
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypothetical+judgment"} hypothetical judgment
                is symbols of the form
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mi 𝒥
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \mathcal{J}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                is a valid context – a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent"} sequent
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒥
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}
                %em typing declarations
                and
                %em equalities
                between terms of the same type. For example, the judgment
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:\mathbb{N}, y:\mathbb{N} \vdash x+y :\mathbb{N}
              %p asserts that any two natural numbers have a sum, which is also a natural number. Similarly,
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi z
                      %mo :
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mi z
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo +
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:\mathbb{N}, y:\mathbb{N}, z:\mathbb{N} \vdash (x+y)+z = x+(y+z)
              %p asserts that natural number addition is associative.
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong rule
                asserts that if some given list of judgments are valid, then so is another one of a specified form derived from them. Of course, to be interesting such rules must contain “meta-variables” which range over contexts, types, or terms. Rules are generally written in the following form:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi Γ
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ⊢
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mn 1
                          %mo :
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mn 1
                          %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                          %mi …
                          %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                          %msub
                            %mi Γ
                            %mi n
                          %mo ⊢
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mi n
                          %mo :
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mi n
                        %mrow
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi s
                          %mo :
                          %mi B
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma_1 \vdash t_1:A_1 \qquad \dots \qquad \Gamma_n \vdash t_n:A_n}{\Delta \vdash s:B}.
              %p
                This is to be read as a rule asserting that if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Γ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_1 \vdash t_1:A_1
                through
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Γ
                        %mi n
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mi n
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_n \vdash t_n:A_n
                are valid judgments, then so is
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mi s
                        %mo :
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta \vdash s:B
          %p A given type theory is determined by its collections of types, judgments, and rules. Rules can of course be classified in various ways; here are some of the most common.
          %h3#structural_rules Structural rules
          %p Structural rules say essentially that variables can be substituted, reordered, and ignored in appropriate ways. For instance, there is an “exchange” structural rule:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo ?
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo ?
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{ \Gamma, x:A, y:B, \Delta \vdash ?}{ \Gamma, y:B, x:A, \Delta \vdash ?}.
          %p
            which asserts that variables in the context can be reordered. (In the presence of dependent types, there is a restriction here that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            cannot depend on
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            Some type theories, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            related to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
          %h3#typeforming_rules Type-forming rules
          %p
            Most of the most interesting rules involve forming new types. For instance, we may want to assert that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            are types then so is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a type,” but we can solve this by treating every
            %em type
            as being itself also a
            %em term
            of a type such as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type
            (which is sometimes written
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma\vdash A:Type \qquad \Gamma \vdash B:Type}{\Gamma \vdash A\times B:Type}.
          %p
            It then comes with attendant rules for forming terms of type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma\vdash A:Type \qquad \Gamma \vdash B:Type}{\Gamma, x:A, y:B \vdash \langle x,y\rangle : A\times B}
          %p and for extracting the original terms out, such as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi t
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi t
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma\vdash A:Type \qquad \Gamma \vdash B:Type}{\Gamma, t:A\times B \vdash \pi_1(t):A} \qquad
                  \\frac{\Gamma\vdash A:Type \qquad \Gamma \vdash B:Type}{\Gamma, t:A\times B \vdash \pi_2(t):B}
          %p
            and the obvious rules saying that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1\langle x,y\rangle = x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2\langle x,y\rangle = y
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \pi_1(t), \pi_2(t)\rangle = t
          %h3#universes Universes
          %p
            Of course, this raises the question—what is the type of
            = succeed "?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Type
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Type
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type = Type_0
            and introduce a hierarchy of additional “universes,” so that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Type
                      %mn 0
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Type
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type_0 : Type_1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Type
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Type
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type_1:Type_2
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type
          %h3#dependent_types_2 Dependent types
          %p As suggested above, we can have types which depend on terms, and type constructors which apply to these. For instance, we can have a rule of dependent product formation:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma, x:A \vdash B(x):Type}{\Gamma \vdash \Pi_{x:A} B(x) : Type}
          %p
            Note that in the case when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is independent of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to B
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{x:A} B(x):Type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B
          %p
            The original dependent type theory was
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+dependent+type+theory"} Martin-Löf dependent type theory
          %h3#propositions Propositions
          %p
            As mentioned above, one way to deal with logic over type theory is to represent a proposition simply by a type, regarded as the type of all its proofs, or of all reasons why it is true. A different way is to introduce a separate type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Prop
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prop
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi Prop
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mo ∀
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi Prop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma, x:A \vdash B(x):Prop}{\Gamma \vdash \forall_{x:A} B(x) : Prop}
          %p
            and similarly
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            becomes
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∃
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
          %p
            In either case, asserting that a proposition is “true” is the same as asserting that it is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+type"} inhabited
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            is a term of type
            = succeed ",\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            can be a proposition and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            a proof or reason why
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is true. In particular, the
            %em axioms
            of a logical theory can also be formulated as term-forming rules.
          %h3#additional_dependencies Additional dependencies
          %p
            It is also possible to have types depending on propositions, propositions depending on propositions, kinds depending on types, etc. etc. See, for instance,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+type+system"} pure type systems
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/calculus+of+constructions"} calculus of constructions
          %h2#typetheoretical_foundations Type-theoretical foundations
          %p
            From a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundational
            point of view, type theory can also be regarded as the language in which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            is written. This has several aspects, notably
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            (the language) and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            (what it means).
          %h3#syntax_of_typetheoretical_foundations Syntax of type-theoretical foundations
          %p
            At the most basic level, what we do when we do mathematics is
            = succeed "." do
              %em manipulate symbols according to specified rules
            %em so
            and not like
            = succeed "," do
              %em so
            %em so
            and not like
            = succeed "." do
              %em so
            = succeed "," do
              %em calculus of terms
            = succeed "." do
              %em type
            = succeed "," do
              %em types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            is a well-formed term of type
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            If we include enough type constructors, then we can use type theory as a foundation for much of mathematics. Instead of building mathematical objects out of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            as in foundational set theories such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\times G\to G
            %em type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            equipped with (among other things) a
            %em term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m(x,y):G
            with free variables
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:G
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y:G
          %h3#type_theory_versus_set_theory Type theory versus set theory
          %p
            Alternately, we could change our terminology so that what we have been calling “types” are instead called “sets”. However, in order for this to accord with the common usages of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"}> set
            ”, we need to include enough type constructors that our types can mimic the behavior of sets, and in particular be “extensional” and have “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+types"}> quotient types
            ”. See the section on
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#ExtensionalIntensional"} Extensional vs Intensional type theory
          %p
            On the other hand, type theory is, among other things, a convenient language for formulating
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+logic"} first-order logical theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundational
            set theories such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Prop
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prop
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:Set
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y:Set
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi Prop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x=y):Prop
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Prop
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x\in y):Prop
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} collection of axioms
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Function
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Prop
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prop
          %p
            Especially when we intend a theory like ZFC or ETCS as a foundation for all of mathematics, it is convenient to call the type-theoretic language in which these theories are written the “meta-language” or “meta-theory,” while ZFC/ETCS is the “object language” or “object theory.” On the other hand, for a more complex and powerful type theory with many type-constructors, which is suitable to serve as a foundation for mathematics itself, it is natural to say that this type theory is
            %em itself
            the
            %em object-theory
            in a meta-theory having meta-types such as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Term
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Term
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Judgment
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Judgment
          %p
            Thus, words like “type” and “set” and “class” are really quite fungible. This sort of level-switch is especially important when we want to study the mathematics
            %em of
            type theory, i.e. the mathematical theory of manipulating symbols according to the rules of type theory, analogous to the mathematical theory of moving pieces around on a chessboard according to the usual rules. When we study type theory in this way, we are
            %em doing
            mathematics, just like when we’re doing group theory or ring theory or whatever. It’s just that our objects of study are called “types”, “terms”, and so on. However, what we do in this mathematical theory
            = succeed "," do
              %em can
          %p Here are some blog discussions about the difference between type theory and set theory:
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/09/towards_a_computeraided_system.html#c026984"} one
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/10/syntax_semantics_and_structura.html#c028459"} two
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2013/01/from_set_theory_to_type_theory.html"} three
          %h3#semantics_of_typetheoretical_foundations Semantics of type-theoretical foundations
          %p
            Now, intuitively, we generally think of a type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as denoting some “collection” of “things”, and a term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t:A
            as indicating a “particular one” of those things. In order for this to make sense, the type theory has to exist in some metatheory (which might or might not be formalized) having a notion of “set” to specify the relevant “collections of things”. In particular, there must be a set of types, and for each type there is a set of terms which can be judged to be of that type. The judgment rules for propositions then become the study of formal logic; we say that a proposition is “provable” or is a “theorem” if it can be judged to be true.
          %p
            Now, a
            %em model
            of this theory (in the category of sets) assigns a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [A]
            (in the meta-theoretic sense) to every type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and a function of appropriate arity to every term, in a way so that the rules and axioms are satisfied. Thus, for instance, a model of Peano arithmetic consists of a set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [N]
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0]\in [N]
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [s]\colon [N]\to[N]
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [Set]
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Prop
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [{\in}]\colon [Set]\times [Set] \to [Prop]
          %p One can then prove, under certain hypotheses, various things about the relationship between syntax and semantics, such as:
          %ul
            %li
              The
              = succeed ":" do
                %em Soundness Theorem
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
              is a proposition which is provable from the axioms of a theory, then the corresponding statement
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\varphi]
              in any model is actually true (in the sense of the metatheory). Equivalently, if a theory has at least one model, then it doesn’t prove a contradiction.
            %li
              The
              = succeed ":" do
                %em Completeness Theorem
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\varphi]
              is true in every model of a theory, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
              is provable in that theory. Equivalently, if a theory doesn’t prove a contradiction, then it has at least one model.
            %li
              The (first)
              = succeed ":" do
                %em Incompleteness Theorem
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
              such that neither
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
              nor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \not\varphi
              is provable in the theory.
            %li Corollary to the completeness and incompleteness theorems: if a theory doesn’t prove a contradiction, then it has more than one model.
          %p
            The “certain hypotheses” is where we get into the difference between
            %em first-order
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em higher-order
            %em higher-order
            if it involves type constructors such as function-types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^A
            (intended to represent the “type of all functions
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P A
            (intended to represent the “type of all subtypes of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %em first-order
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Prop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prop
            specially in first-order logic. If we actually have a type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Prop
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prop
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Prop
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prop \cong P 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Prop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prop
            to be a ”kind“ on the same level as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type
          %p
            The Soundness Theorem is true for all theories, but
            = succeed "." do
              %em the Completeness Theorem is true only for first-order theories
            %em categorical
            in the logician’s sense: having exactly one model (at least, up to isomorphism). The second-order version of Peano Arithmetic has this property. (At this level, there is little fundamental difference between first-order and higher-order theories; they each have advantages and disadvantages. However, when we move up to the metalevel and talk about the term calculus itself, we always get a first-order theory. This is why some people believe that first-order logic is the only truly “foundational” logic.)
          %h3#term_models Term models
          %p
            One usually proves the Completeness Theorem by building a “tautological” model out of the theory itself. That is, for each type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            we simply take the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [A]
            to be the set of terms of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            with no free variables (or “ground terms”). However, without modification, this naive idea fails for two reasons.
          %p First of all, there might not be enough ground terms. Some of the axioms of the theory might assert that there exists something with some property, without there being a corresponding term constructor actually producing something with that property. This is obviously the case for the usual version of ZFC, which has no term constructors at all (hence no ground terms at all!) but lots of axioms that assert the existence of things. This problem is easily remedied, however, by introducing new constant terms or term constructors into the language.
          %p
            The second problem is that we may not know how to define all the necessary relations on the ground terms in order to have a model. Suppose, for instance, we have a couple of ground terms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_2
            in some augmented version of ZFC; how can we tell whether
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_1\in t_2
            should hold in our tautological model? Certainly if the axioms of the theory imply
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_1\in t_2
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∉
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_1\notin t_2
          %p
            This works, but the model we get (though small, even countable, and concrete) isn’t really
            = succeed ";" do
              %em canonical
            %em precisely
            those that are derivable in the theory.
          %p
            In the case of Peano Arithmetic, we can avoid introducing new constant terms and obtain a model which is “canonical” and in fact the “smallest” in some sense: it consists of the terms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi …
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi …
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(s(\dots(s(0))\dots))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            be a sentence independent of ZF; then our term-constructor for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+separation"} axiom of separation
            gives us a term
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\emptyset | \varphi\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset \in \{\emptyset | \varphi\}
            hold in the term model? We have to make an arbitrary choice.
          %p
            (It
            %em is
            true that any
            %em given
            model of ZF contains a
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                minimal model
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/minimal+model+of+ZF"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+set"} transitive set
            which is a model of ZF. However, different models of ZF have different minimal models, and the construction of the minimal model is not “syntactic” in this sense.)
          %p
            The slicker categorial approach described above using categories of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contexts"} contexts
            does produce a really canonical model, but only with an expanded notion of “model”: instead of each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [A]
            being a set, we take it to be an object of some fixed category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            with enough structure. We can then build a much more “tautological” model because we have the freedom to build the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
            along with the model. In the resulting model, the true statements are
            %em precisely
            the statements provable in the theory, and it’s even initial among all models of the theory in the appropriate sort of category.
